Verified against HEAD's code, not just read: I extracted the original `decodeTrailResource` verbatim and ran it over the relevant bodies (scratch test since removed).

**On the default backend (legacy), the case couldn't arise.** `findTrailByNumber` began with `if !isEntireAPITrailClient(client)` and did a list scan matching `t.Number == number`. A miss returned `nil, nil` naturally — the number route was never requested.

**On the opt-in `entire-api` backend, it returned a phantom.** A 2xx body that decoded to a zero-value resource fell through to the `{trail:{…}}` envelope branch. Any JSON *object* without a `trail` key unmarshals into that struct without error, leaving `Trail` zero — so the function returned `&<all-zero TrailResource>` with a nil error:

```
{"trails":[{"id":"trl_a","number":1}],"total":2}  -> err=<nil>  {ID: Number:0}
{}                                               -> err=<nil>  {ID: Number:0}
{"error":"not found"}                            -> err=<nil>  {ID: Number:0}
[{"id":"trl_a"}]                                 -> err=decode trail resource: ... cannot unmarshal array
{"trail":{"id":"x"}}                             -> err=<nil>  {ID:x Number:0}
```

Only a non-object body errored, and that error came from the *first* unmarshal — the envelope branch never rejected anything an object could produce.

So the guard I added doesn't change any case the old code got right; it converts that phantom into `nil, nil`. It matters because `resolveTrailBySelector` treats `found != nil` as success, so a numeric selector (`entire trail show 575`, checkout, resume, review) would have rendered an empty trail instead of reporting not-found. `runTrailDelete` only read `found.Title` from it, so there it degraded to an empty title.

This is also exactly what the `TestResolveTrailBySelector_FindsBySelector/by_number` failure showed mid-refactor: its stub answers every path with a list response, and the by-number lookup came back as `&api.TrailResource{ID:"", Number:0, …}`. Since the default backend list-scanned, nobody hit it in practice unless they had set `ENTIRE_TRAILS_BACKEND=entire-api`.

